7592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306508} {:answer/id #uuid "fdfc4450-53a4-5b3d-aa90-d02623cc7658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdfc4450-53a4-5b3d-aa90-d02623cc7658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307495} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbbab3a0-5fb1-5581-9325-630ba726fe89" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ea8ee90-f780-5300-8262-a562939b4083" :question/math-academy-id "q-333141" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[2,\\text{ }-1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/1d/1d2a232b4351c84871d89ba25384aa53777760355ceea6458b07fba9c0a9ed78.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/12/122759a459c8dd56dbe69c44c0da4ccffbbc660465fbedc7457e17b42430436e.png)\n\nFinally, since the dominant eigenvalue is \n{{field-9}},\n solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector \n{{field-10}} \nas they move away from the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/fa/fa5033f974e501bb8bf6960753b1706569f5ed59c3dec6d22e8b200317f2a487.png)" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/1d/1d2a232b4351c84871d89ba25384aa53777760355ceea6458b07fba9c0a9ed78.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=0\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/12/122759a459c8dd56dbe69c44c0da4ccffbbc660465fbedc7457e17b42430436e.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n\n$$\n0<{\\lambda }_{2}<{\\lambda }_{1}\n$$\n\nthus, ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ is the dominant eigenvalue.\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$  solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector  ${v}_{1}$ as they move away from the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/fc/fcaa3ba63a1e6ad061eecf1f72caf3c9f8ba78b014eff044ee5be682e1325128.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166917 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13af6d27-628d-5bdb-b396-299d9cfd64e7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Parameters of a System Given Its Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ebb7247-d920-5f19-a054-f3be245ae329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305758} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2587d1-2c35-5630-8a3f-2a3d766f0b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ebb7247-d920-5f19-a054-f3be245ae329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305758} :answer-field/id #uuid "048f75b0-3308-58d6-8fd0-7540aece1c8d" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307534} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59cdbe25-38ed-5096-bb22-b8966bd7a828" :answer/type {:db/id #ed